Low-Level Technical & Verifier Analysis
1. Pre-TLS Interception State Management (financial_ssl_uprobe.bpf.c)

    Thread Safety: Using tgid_pid (bpf_get_current_pid_tgid()) as the hash key in active_read_buffers correctly isolates concurrent SSL_read calls across multi-threaded trading engines.

    Payload Truncation: Payload lengths are bounded to MAX_FIN_PAYLOAD (4096 bytes) before calling bpf_probe_read_user(), ensuring BPF verifier loop and stack bounds are strictly satisfied.

2. XDP Packet Slicing (xdp_cns_mirror.bpf.c)

    Direct Packet Access Bounds: The loop copy bounds check (payload + i + 1) <= data_end ensures every byte dereference from the XDP frame is verifier-safe without causing packet-pointer invalidation faults.

    Loop Unrolling: Loop unrolling (#pragma unroll) satisfies older eBPF verifiers lacking bounded loop support.

3. Database Hook Pointer Offset (regsho_db_audit.bpf.c)

    Struct Offset Alignment: bpf_probe_read_user(&source_text_ptr, sizeof(source_text_ptr), (void *)((char *)queryDesc + 8)) targets QueryDesc->sourceText. In PostgreSQL internals:
    C

    typedef struct QueryDesc {
        CmdType operation;        /* 4 bytes */
        /* 4 bytes padding on x86_64 */
        PlannedStmt *plannedstmt; /* offset 8 */
        const char *sourceText;   /* offset 16 on standard x86_64 Postgres builds */
        ...
    } QueryDesc;

    (Note: Verify struct layout and compiler padding on your specific target postgres binary build to confirm whether sourceText resides at offset 8 or 16).

4. Stack Trace Context in Uprobes (pco_state_audit.bpf.c)

    Tracing Context: bpf_get_stack(ctx, ...) relies on BPF_UPROBE macro expanding ctx as the underlying struct pt_regs *. Setting BPF_F_USER_STACK extracts the user-space call frame accurately up to 16 frames.

